A||N||8||stimpy||A ? for the speaker geeks.||||5||
Z||000000||stimpy||04-02-2000||10:53 PM||||Just put in a 6" Bazooka tube under my dash on passenger side, powered by 200PL Phase Linear amp. The amp has a built-in crossover but if I run it without the x-over put in the "sub" position, it makes the whole system sound so much better...like a center channel. Still has all the bass I get with the switch on sub.<BR>My ? is this, while I know that a big subwill fry with higher freqs, will this 6.5" woofer get screwed up by running a full signal?<BR>Enquiring minds need to know.<BR>||||reg||5
Z||000001||Ozzy||04-03-2000||12:46 AM||||No...<P>If a speaker is designed to handle low frequencies at power levels like 160 Hz an lower, then nothing else frequency wise will harm it.<P>Ozzy<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000002||Raydar||04-03-2000||12:50 AM||||Never heard of higher frequencies frying a sub. As far as I know, the sub will just not be able to reproduce them, due to the mass of the cone. (The 6 1/2 will probably go fairly high.)<BR>On the other hand, bass frequencies (or harmonics caused by distortion of bass frequencies) can blow mids, and especially tweeters, into the middle of next week. <BR>If you hear distortion, whether it's coming from your speakers or amp, something is operating outside of its design limits. <BR>Kinda long winded. Hope this helps.  <BR> <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000003||lowCG||04-03-2000||01:01 AM||||You'll just experience some horrible sound.<BR>The speaker probably already produces some mid-bass/vocals even with the low pass;filters can't keep it all out,but if you use any higher than a 50hz cut,it'll sound lousy with a tinny sounding bass note.||||reg||1
Z||000004||stimpy||04-03-2000||06:17 AM||||Thanks for the info!||||reg||1
Z||000005||Formula||04-03-2000||04:00 PM||||If you hook it up as a tweeter it will fry it.  personal experience||||reg||1
Z||000006||Patrick's Dad||04-03-2000||10:39 PM||||What I would expect is muddy mids. A woofer will naturally rolloff anything it can't move fast enough to repoduce (For a 6½", this should be about 500-1000 Hz, maybe lower in a ported tube.).<P>On the other hand, if your amp is trying too hard to put signal into the sub (Especially as impedance drops to near short circuit levels with heavy bass), your amp will clip, meaning it spends a fraction of each peak flatlining voltage. This direct current is energizing the voice coil (An electromagnet) without movement (Which ventilates it. Frying a speaker becomes a literal.<P>The dynamic is similar to the car. A car is not a static load. Depending on your input (Pedal/Volume), your speaker becomes a varying load for its amlifier (engine and transmission), its spider and surround (suspension), and its ferrofluid, if any (coolant). A speaker, of course, doesn't have brakes, but the analogy follows more as engine braking to control your car's speed and handling, so an amplifier's damping factor helps control the cone's movement. So, just as it's easy to blow up an underpowered car, it's just as easy to blow up an underpowered sound system. A previous poster blew a woofer by hooking it up as a tweeter; possibly by hooking it to the wrong side of a crossover. The net effect was not enough coolong. The cone couldn't move fast enough to reproduce the tones, nor could it move enough distance to cool itself off. Goodbye voice coil.<P>Hope this helps.<P>::::Boy, talk about long winded::::||||reg||1
Z||000007||olympic||04-03-2000||11:07 PM||||   A 6.5" speaker isn't really big enough to be a sub woofer.  The crossover in the amp is probably centered at 80 Hz and your woofer probably  cant reproduce the frequencies below 50-60 Hz too well.  By turning the crossover off, yout sub is getting more frequencies it CAN reproduce giving you louder and fuller sound.  It's working more like a midrange woofer.<P>     Anyways, high frequencies wont hurt your woofer.  It simply cant reproduce them.  If your happy the way it sounds, just leave it that way.||||reg||1
Z||000008||stimpy||04-04-2000||07:38 AM||||I do appreciate all responses.  I do believe, however, that the great SAS Bazooka/Fiero experiment is a bust.  I did elect to run it on the sub circuit and still wound up cooking it. I really wasn't even punishing it that hard! Got a replacementwth the admonition from the salesguy that I "better not blow it again, that would be abuse." therefor not covered in their exchange policy. Driving home last night I start smelling hot speaker again. So the sub gets traded back for cash today.  I will be looking for some Q-Logic pods to mount my old=school Altec-Lansing 6.5" coax. Now those really hit!||||reg||1
